EXT. SALT FLATS WAGON STOP - NOON

White ground. White sky. A single wagon with a cracked wheel. A water barrel under a tarp. The heat shimmers like paper held over a flame.

HANNAH DELACROIX (30s, dust-caked bonnet, rifle across her knees) sits on the wagon tongue. Her husband EZEKIEL "ZEKE" DELACROIX (30s, bandaged hand) paces the barrel. Across the flats, a lone rider approaches slowly, leading a second horse: MARSHAL TOBIAS GRAINGER (50s, gray duster, tin star scratched with age).

Grainger reins up, tips his hat.

GRAINGER
Afternoon.

HANNAH
Marshal.

ZEKE
Afternoon.

GRAINGER
Looks like a wheel gave out.

HANNAH
It did.

GRAINGER
I could help.

ZEKE
We're fine.

Grainger swings down. He pats his horse. He looks at the barrel, the rifle, Zeke's bandaged hand.

GRAINGER
(to Zeke)
Cut yourself?

ZEKE
Rope.

GRAINGER
Funny. Rope burns look different from teeth.

Hannah's finger moves toward the trigger. Zeke's jaw works.

HANNAH
Marshal, you're welcome to water.

GRAINGER
That's kind. I'm not thirsty.

He walks to the back of the wagon and lifts the tarp. Underneath: a canvas sack, dark with dried stains.

GRAINGER (CONT'D)
(to himself)
Well, now.

ZEKE
Let it be, Marshal.

HANNAH
Let it be.

They say it together, flat and fast.

ZEKE (CONT'D)
^We didn't kill him for the money.

HANNAH
^He came for the children.

Grainger lets the tarp fall. The wind lifts a thin curtain of salt.

GRAINGER
(to the flats)
Hell of a place to find out you were right.

He unbuckles his gun belt and drops it in the dust.

GRAINGER (CONT'D)
(to Hannah)
Put that rifle down, ma'am. I'm only going to sit.

She doesn't. He sits anyway.
